<p>Guar gum is unusual among our product lines in that it serves two genuinely distinct buyer types under a single HS code. Food manufacturers use it as a natural thickener and stabiliser; oil and gas companies use industrial-grade guar gum as a key component in hydraulic fracturing (fracking) fluids. Both draw on the same guar seed crop, refined to different purity and viscosity specifications — and both show up in the same US import figures. The United States is the clear largest single market for Indian guar gum, and because India is the dominant world producer and exporter, the figures below represent a large share of total global trade, not just India's share of a market others also supply significantly. This guide is for US food-ingredient and oilfield buyers sourcing either grade.</p>

<!-- slot:facts -->

