<p>The US paid about 9% more than India's world average and about 14% more than Russia. A unit value averages every contract and is not a price, but the gap fits the mix of US buyers. Two segments share the HS code. Oilfield service companies and frac-fluid blenders around Houston buy fast-hydrating powder, often in jumbo bags, and judge it by how quickly the gel builds and how it responds to borate crosslinkers. Food-ingredient distributors and manufacturers buy food grade to the Food Chemicals Codex specification that 21 CFR 184.1339 requires, in 25kg bags, with FDA paperwork. Both carry processing and testing costs that the plain thickener grades sold to some other markets do not.</p>
<p>Scenario: a Houston frac-fluid blender and a New Jersey dairy-ingredient distributor both ask for "guar gum, 5000 cps". The first needs a timed hydration reading in its own fluid; the second needs microbiology and a GRAS-compliant specification. Same HS code, different product. Our <a href="/blog/guar-gum-food-vs-industrial-grade/">food vs industrial grade guide</a> sets out the specifications for each.</p>
<!-- /insight -->

<h2>Which grade does your US application call for?</h2>
<p>Grade is the single most important thing to get right on a guar gum enquiry, and the two are not interchangeable. Both are milled from guar split, the endosperm of the seed, which is mostly galactomannan, but food grade is tested to a food-additive specification (JECFA's INS 412 and, in the US, the Food Chemicals Codex) while oilfield grade is tested on performance. Tell us your end use upfront, and if you need the parameter-by-parameter detail, start with the grade guide linked above rather than a generic data sheet.</p>
<h2>How should a US buyer specify viscosity and mesh?</h2>
<p>Within either grade, viscosity is what matters most. Measured in centipoise (cP) under specified test conditions, it is the parameter that determines how guar gum performs in its application. Many US labs read it on a Brookfield rotational viscometer, so name the model, spindle, speed, concentration and hydration time, and ask us to test the same way. Mesh size (80, 100, 200 or 300) is the secondary specification: fast-hydrating oilfield grades sit at the finer end. Give us your target viscosity range and mesh, and we'll quote against your actual technical requirement.</p>

<!-- insight:container-economics -->
<h2>How much guar gum fits in a 20ft container to Houston?</h2>
<p>About 18-20 MT in 25kg bags in a 20ft, and 21-24 MT in a 40ft. Guar gum powder is dense enough that both boxes reach their weight limit before they are full. At the midpoints, 22.5 MT against 19 MT, a 40ft carries only about 18% more. It is the cheaper box per tonne only if its freight rate is under about 1.18 times the 20ft rate to Houston, Savannah or Los Angeles/Long Beach. Divide each rate by its tonnage before you book.</p>
<p>At the 2024 US average of USD 1.89/kg, a 19 MT 20ft carries about USD 35,900 of guar gum, packed as 760 bags of 25kg or 19 jumbo bags. Transit runs 20-28 days, so a Houston blender ordering for a drilling programme needs to place the order about six weeks before the powder is needed, allowing for 10-15 days ex-factory.</p>
<!-- /insight -->

<!-- slot:docs -->

<p>The certificate of analysis covers purity as well as viscosity, mesh and moisture. The FDA food-import documentation requirement applies to food-grade shipments on the US import side; industrial-grade shipments typically don't carry that requirement, which is one more reason to state your end use before documents are drafted.</p>

<!-- insight:documentation-failures -->
<p><strong>Where food-grade US entries get held.</strong> Under FSMA, the US importer of food, the owner or consignee, must run a Foreign Supplier Verification Program (FSVP): a hazard analysis for the food, an evaluation of the supplier and verification such as testing or record review. The importer's name, email and unique facility identifier (a DUNS number is accepted) must be on each food line when the entry is filed with US Customs and Border Protection (CBP). Separately, FDA prior notice must be filed and confirmed at least 8 hours before a food shipment arriving by water reaches the port, under 21 CFR 1.279, and not more than 30 days before arrival when filed through ABI/ACE. Scenario: a first food-grade container to Savannah is booked by a distributor whose FSVP file is still being written. The documents we send are complete, but the entry stalls because the importer's side is not ready. Ask your importer to confirm FSVP status and who files prior notice before we load. FDA also keeps import alerts, under which shipments from a listed firm can be detained without physical examination. Check whether an import alert lists guar gum or your supplier before you place a first order.</p>
<!-- /insight -->

