<h2>What actually determines the price you're quoted</h2>
<p>Guar gum is one of the more genuinely volatile commodities we handle, and we don't quote a flat per-kilogram number without your specification because the market itself doesn't move on a flat basis. Five factors matter most: the size of that year's Rajasthan guar-seed crop (guar is monsoon-dependent, and a weak monsoon in the Bikaner-Jodhpur-Hanumangarh belt tightens raw-seed supply and pushes prices up across the board), US shale-drilling activity specifically (industrial-grade demand tracks rig counts and fracking activity, which move with energy prices — a genuinely unusual demand driver for an agricultural export), viscosity grade (higher-viscosity industrial specs and tighter food-grade tolerances both command a premium over standard grades), mesh size (finer mesh costs more to process), and prevailing INR/USD exchange rates and Gulf Coast freight rates.</p>
<p>The shale-drilling link deserves a word for food-grade buyers in particular. Industrial-grade demand is linked, at least partly, to US oil and gas drilling activity — rig counts and fracking activity levels move with energy prices, which in turn affects guar gum demand and pricing cycles in a way that's disconnected from food-industry demand entirely. If you're a food-grade buyer, understand that guar gum pricing can move for reasons that have nothing to do with food-ingredient markets.</p>
<p>The annual series shows the size of those swings. India's guar gum exports were worth USD 339.4 million in 2021, USD 508.1 million in 2022, USD 401.4 million in 2023 and USD 417.3 million in 2024 (WITS/UN Comtrade). That is a 50% rise in one year, followed by a 21% fall. For a US food manufacturer, the practical answer is contract length: a 12-month price agreed at the top of a cycle can look expensive within months, and one agreed at the bottom can be hard to renew. One answer is shorter contracts timed around the new seed crop from Jodhpur and the wider Rajasthan belt, which reaches mills from October.</p>
<p>Quotes are issued FOB, CIF or CFR against your specific grade, viscosity range, mesh size, packaging and destination port — never as a flat market-average rate. To get an accurate quote fast, tell us: food-grade or industrial-grade, target viscosity (cP) and mesh, packaging preference, quantity, and destination port.</p>
