<h2>What goes wrong on a first order, and how to avoid it</h2>
<p>The most common technical dispute on guar gum shipments is a viscosity-reading mismatch between buyer and supplier labs. Viscosity is measured under specific instrument, temperature, shear-rate and hydration-time conditions, and two labs testing the same sample under slightly different protocols can report meaningfully different cP values — this isn't usually fraud, it's a methodology gap. Specify the test method and conditions in your purchase order, not just a target cP number, and ask what method our certificate of analysis uses so both sides are reading the same test.</p>
<p>The second common issue is moisture affecting viscosity readings after the fact — guar gum is hygroscopic, and moisture pickup during a humid transit or improper storage changes both weight and hydration performance. We pack in sealed, moisture-resistant packaging specifically to manage this, and we'd recommend confirming any supplier's packaging spec before ordering rather than assuming it's standard.</p>
<p>Third, EU-bound shipments specifically require a SHEFEXIL-endorsed PCP (pentachlorophenol) analytical report — a mandatory testing requirement that followed a 2012 industry-wide contamination episode. This doesn't apply to US shipments, but it's worth knowing if you're comparing quotes across markets, since a supplier's EU compliance process tells you something about how seriously they take contamination testing generally.</p>
<p>What first-time US buyers most often get wrong: treating food-grade and industrial-grade guar gum as the same product with different price points, when they're functionally separate products with different purity, viscosity and documentation requirements. A quote for one is not comparable to a quote for the other, and asking for "guar gum" without specifying grade is the single most common cause of quote confusion we see on this product.</p>
